\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For an entire function $f$, write
\[
 M(r,f)=\max_{|z|=r}|f(z)|,\qquad m_0(r,f)=\min_{|z|=r}|f(z)|.
\]
For nonconstant $f$, its order is $\rho(f)=\limsup_{r\to\infty}\log\log M(r,f)/\log r$;
the lower order is defined using $\liminf$ instead. Every constant entire function, including the zero function, has order and lower order zero. In logarithmic modulus ratios, use the extended-real convention $\log0=-\infty$.
Let $\rho\ge0$ be finite, with $\rho\notin\{n/2:n=1,2,\ldots\}$. Entire functions $f,g$ are linearly independent if no nonzero pair $u,v\in\mathbb C$ satisfies $uf+vg\equiv0$. Their Wronskian is $W(f,g)=fg'-gf'$.

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Can linearly independent entire functions with $\rho(f)=\rho(g)=\rho$ have
\[\rho\bigl(W(f,g)\bigr)<\rho?\] \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}
Update 2.25 corrects an older ``distinct'' formulation to ``linearly independent'' and reports Eremenko's negative answer. The positive half-integer orders are explicitly excluded in the recorded question. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can the Wronskian grow with strictly smaller order than two independent entire functions of the same finite order, when that order is not a positive half-integer?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2302025 (AMR-022-2025). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] W. K. Hayman and E. F. Lingham, \emph{Research Problems in Function Theory} (2018), Chapter 2, Problem 2.25 and Update 2.25. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}.
\end{description}
